WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:06.069
Super Mario Odyssey isn't just Mario's biggest
adventure ever, or one of the best title of

00:00:06.069 --> 00:00:11.360
2017: it's also a giant, sprawling love letter
to the portly plumber's video game history.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.129
Odyssey might take Mario further than he's
ever been from the Mushroom Kingdom, but the

00:00:15.129 --> 00:00:20.120
entire journey is absolutely drenched in callbacks
to his long and storied past.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:24.360
Just watch out for spoilers: this list goes
into just a few of the big surprises waiting

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:25.410
for you.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.250
A wonderful wardrobe

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:31.270
Super Mario Odyssey lets you buy a variety
of different outfits with your hard-earned

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:32.270
coins.

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:36.609
That's right: if you've gotten tired of Mario's
red and blue getup — or, if after 36 years,

00:00:36.609 --> 00:00:40.890
you figure he's simply due for a change of
clothes — you can give Mario a makeover.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:44.890
Some of the costumes are brand new to the
Mario series, but others have pretty obvious

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:45.890
origins.

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:50.809
Luigi, Wario, and Waluigi's outfits are all
available for purchase, and Mario's "Classic

00:00:50.809 --> 00:00:55.510
Suit" makes his overalls red and his shirt
blue, just like in the very first Super Mario

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:56.510
Bros.

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:00.910
You'll also find some fairly obscure stuff
from Super Mario history: the "Chef Suit"

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:03.639
recalls Mario's days baking in Yoshi's Cookie.

00:01:03.639 --> 00:01:08.350
The safari-ready "Explorer Suit" is a nod
to the Game Boy puzzler Mario's Picross, while

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:13.250
the "Poncho Costume" resembles Mario's outfit
in the Game Boy edition of Qix, as well as

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:16.190
one of his get-ups in his Japan-only anime
appearance.

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:21.130
The "Painter Outfit" references the Super
Nintendo title Mario Paint, the patriotic

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:25.790
"Golf Outfit" is from Open Tournament Golf,
while the "Aviator Outfit" sort of looks like

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:30.520
the clothes Mario wears in his first portable
outing, Super Mario Land, during that game's

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:31.810
shoot 'em up plane levels.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:36.570
It goes even deeper, though, and unless you
know Mario's Japanese heritage, a few of these

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:38.530
are especially deep cuts.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:44.280
Mario's "Mechanic Outfit" is borrowed from
1988's Famicom Grand Prix II: 3D Hot Rally,

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.510
a Japan-only racing game.

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:52.160
Mario's "Fashionable Outfit" isn't an old
reference, but it's still weirdly obscure.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:56.520
In 2014, when Nintendo released its New 3DS,
it launched a Japanese advertising campaign

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:01.390
starring singer Kyary Pamyu Pamyu in which
Nintendo's classic characters get brand new

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:01.890
clothes.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:07.920
Look familiar?

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.789
Celebrating Super Mario Maker

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:15.500
In 2015, Nintendo rolled out the last 2D Mario
game that anyone would ever need: Super Mario

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:16.500
Maker.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.710
The new title flipped the script and let players
officially design and share their own Mario

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:22.400
levels for the first time.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:27.260
And despite appearing on the underperforming
Wii U, Super Mario Maker sold over a million

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:33.120
copies and boasts over 7.2 million levels
for Mario fans to enjoy… or be impossibly

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:34.150
frustrated by.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:38.280
By any measure, that's a hit — and one that
should keep fans and amateur game designers

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.320
busy for years to come.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:44.860
Naturally, Nintendo put a few carefully-placed
Super Mario Maker Easter eggs in Odyssey,

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:48.880
like Mario's yellow and red "Builder Helmet
and Outfit," which can be purchased in New

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:50.540
Donk City's hat shop.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:54.150
Super Mario Odyssey's other Mario Maker egg
is a little more subtle.

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:59.470
In some levels, you'll run across 8-bit pictures
of Mario and Princess Peach dressed in cat

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:00.470
outfits.

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:04.970
While the cat suit first appeared in the Wii
U's Super Mario 3D World, the sprites themselves

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:09.260
are from Super Mario Maker, appearing as one
of the costumes that Mario can wear.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:13.810
In order to unlock the costumes in Super Mario
Maker, you'll need to clear a special event.

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:18.110
In Super Mario Odyssey, the sprites serve
a different purpose: hit them with Cappy,

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:21.750
Mario's new hat-like friend, and you'll get
a small health boost.

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:24.340
Mario 64, evolved

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:29.060
Odyssey owes more to Super Mario 64 than any
other Mario game, since both drop players

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:31.500
into wide open worlds filled with collectibles.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:36.310
Platforming is still Mario's bread and butter,
but both of those games focus more on exploration

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:37.790
than mechanical challenges.

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:40.270
The games are connected by more than just
that, though.

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:44.140
Dorrie, the goggles-wearing Loch Ness Monster-like
creature who floats around the Lake Kingdom

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:48.650
made his debut in Super Mario 64, in which
players could ride him around in order to

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:50.360
discover new areas.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:54.850
In the Wooded Kingdom, Mario needs to chase
down some terrified rabbits, not unlike a

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:59.489
series of similar rabbit-based chases that
take place in Princess Peach's basement in

00:03:59.489 --> 00:04:01.160
Super Mario 64.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:05.849
And while Mario stomps his way through various
kingdoms, he runs across paintings that transport

00:04:05.849 --> 00:04:10.920
him to other worlds, which is a pretty clear
reference to Super Mario 64's hanging portraits,

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:13.500
which serve as gateways to that game's levels.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:19.250
And if there was any doubt, Mario's goofy
low-def Mario 64 outfit is in here too.

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:21.340
All Kong, all the time

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:26.480
Most Mario fans know that Nintendo's mascot
first appeared in the company's 1981 hit arcade

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:31.520
game Donkey Kong, in which the mustachioed
construction worker had to rescue his girlfriend,

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:33.520
Pauline, from the big, dumb animal.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:37.440
The game's New Donk City level is one giant
tribute to the 8-bit ape.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:41.970
Pauline, once a damsel in distress, is now
New Donk City's beloved, singing mayor.

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:47.030
The city is also full of moving girders, and
intrepid explorers will find wooden barrels,

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:49.199
just like the kind Donkey Kong used to throw.

00:04:49.199 --> 00:04:53.580
New Donk City's streets are also named after
characters from the Donkey Kong Country series,

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:58.410
including relatively obscure ones like Donkey
Kong's animal pals Rambi and Rattly.

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:03.120
Cranky Kong, who is canonically the original
Donkey Kong, gets his own avenue as well.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:08.490
New Donk City license plates read "1981-ND,"
referring to Donkey Kong's release date.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:12.940
And Super Mario Odyssey's jazzy title track,
"Jump Up, Super Star!", uses the original

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:15.200
Donkey Kong music as a backing melody.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:16.600
And let's not forget about this.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:21.470
When Mario first appeared in Donkey Kong,
he was known simply as "Jumpman".

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:23.250
You found a Power Moon!

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:28.139
Every time Mario finds a Power Moon, Super
Mario Odyssey's main collectible, Mario celebrates

00:05:28.139 --> 00:05:30.460
with a victory pose, but they're not all the
same.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:32.770
Sometimes, he puts up two fingers.

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:35.560
Sometimes, he triumphantly pumps his fist
in the air.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:38.000
Occasionally, he keeps his hand open.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.720
Unsurprisingly, there's a little bit of history
behind each one of those poses.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:46.910
The split-fingered V-sign is what Mario does
after he finds a Star in Super Mario 64.

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:50.100
The fist pump hails from the two Super Mario
Galaxy games.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:53.180
The open-handed pose comes from Super Mario
Sunshine.

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:57.600
It's a small touch, but it goes a long way
towards honoring Super Mario Odyssey's predecessors

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:01.360
— after all, Mario wouldn't be where he
is without 'em.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:03.110
Toe-tapping tunes

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:07.350
While Super Mario Odyssey is full of visual
nods to the series' past, the game is also

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:11.210
full of auditory callbacks to past Super Mario
games.

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:15.620
For example, Mario runs across boom boxes
that pump out smooth jazzy covers of classic

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:16.620
Mario tracks.

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:20.590
If you use Cappy to possess the statues in
the Sand Kingdom, you'll notice that they

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:23.970
hum little snippets of past Super Mario soundtracks.

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:25.880
Return to the Mushroom Kingdom

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:28.300
Odyssey is a big game with lots to do.

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:32.520
And of course, as with most Mario games, beating
Bowser is just the beginning.

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:36.650
Once you defeat King Koopa and free Princess
Peach, the real challenge begins.

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:40.810
Not only is every world flooded with a new
set of Power Moons to hunt down, but Mario

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:45.970
can access a few new kingdoms — including
one that looks very, very familiar: the Mushroom

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:46.970
Kingdom.

00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:51.880
Just like in Mario 64, Mario's home base is
full of things to do, with 104 more Power

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:56.720
Moons to discover, which is just under Mario
64's 120 stars.

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:01.230
You can access new challenges through paintings,
find Yoshi hiding on the castle grounds, and

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:05.870
even discover a few areas that look exactly
like their 64-bit counterparts.

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:08.030
The music is even the same as the original.

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:12.440
In addition, in a fun nostalgic throwback,
the Mushroom Kingdom's Power Moons look like

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:14.669
Mario 64's bright yellow stars.

00:07:14.669 --> 00:07:17.620
Princess Peach may not be around, but it doesn't
really matter.

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:20.730
After all this time, it's just good to be
home.

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:21.730
"Wee-hoo!"

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:23.250
Thanks for watching!

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:26.130
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:28.370
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

